      "id": "increment.v11-terminal-release-scope-alignment",
      "state": "active",
      "correctionClass": "terminal-release-authorization-scope-alignment",
      "observedProblem": "Current O5 requires the named human to accept cross-host and cross-operating-system equivalence, conformance-contract and adaptive-projection fitness, privacy treatment, exact tested operating-system scope and bounded claims, but the code-owned terminal tag annotation still fixes acceptedScope to the predecessor normative-profile and thin-reference-adapters vocabulary under the old v1 format. A weak source validator could therefore let terminal acceptance omit current form and operating-system scope, while a strict validator could not express the current acceptance contract.",
      "hypothesis": "Versioning the terminal authorization annotation and replacing its exact code-owned acceptedScope with the current O5 human-authority dimensions will make the final release gate reject predecessor scope and require current form-neutral and bounded cross-operating-system authorization without changing outcome thresholds, cohort state, private evidence or release mechanics.",
      "falsifier": "Stop or revise if the repair adds a new outcome, threshold, human decision, publication action or release artifact; prebuilds a task outcome or terminal source validator; changes the frozen profile, cohort protocol, environment-attribution contract or cohort authorization; accepts the predecessor scope under the new format; or causes any current O1-O5 progress.",
      "stopCondition": "Stop after the code-owned terminal annotation format and acceptedScope exactly cover current O5 equivalence, portable semantics, conformance contract, adaptive projections, privacy, tested host/OS/runtime/virtualization scope, claim ceiling, candidate, tag and public release; predecessor scope is rejected in a focused regression; verifier and full product tests pass with O1-O5 at 0/5; all task-created residue is absent; and this outcome-neutral increment exits the current graph.",
